The structured data from this hospitalization include 5 imaging reports; examination types: electrocardiogram, PET/CT, and inpatient CT; summary of conclusions: electrocardiogram within normal limits; 1, status post resection of the pancreatic tail and spleen, with a high-density shadow visible in the operative area and no abnormal hypermetabolic foci identified;
2, retroperitoneal para-aortic lymph nodes below the left renal vein are visualized, without high metabolism; regular follow-up is recommended;
3, a cord-like shadow in the subcutaneous tissue of the left lower abdominal wall with mildly increased focal abdominal wall metabolism, considered possibly postoperative change;
4, calcified foci in the bilateral basal ganglia regions;
5, increased metabolism in the bilateral oropharynx; bilateral submandibular and level II cervical lymph nodes are visualized, with mildly increased metabolism, considered possibly inflammatory lesions;
6, small nodules in both lungs, one of which is located in the apical segment of the right upper lobe, without high metabolism; follow-up is recommended; patchy and cord-like foci in both lungs; lymph nodes in mediastinal regions 4R, 4L, and 7, and in both hila are visualized, with mildly increased metabolism, considered possibly inflammatory lesions;
7, right renal cyst; calcified nodular focus in the right paracolic gutter;
8, enlarged prostate with calcified foci; calcified focus in the right scrotum;
9, increased metabolism in the soft tissues adjacent to both femoral necks, considered possibly inflammatory lesions; degenerative changes of the spine. After fasting for more than 6h, whole-body PET/CT tomographic imaging was performed following intravenous injection of 18F-FDG, with clear images. The examination range extended from the vertex to the upper thigh at 1/3. PET scan: head: 5min*1 bed positions; neck to thigh: 1.5min*7 bed positions. Several tiny nodules in both lungs and a calcified focus in the right lung; slight bilateral pleural thickening; deep venous catheter in place. Please correlate with the clinical findings, medical history, and relevant examinations, and follow up. Prostatic calcification; a small amount of patchy, flocculent opacity in the left lower subcutaneous soft tissue of the pelvis. Please correlate with the clinical findings and other examinations, and follow up.
